From 9ac5eb3d291f1a0a7bdba91f88a4c8fde73310e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Roscoe <bryanroscoe@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:07:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] AI: score free-for-all attacks as a whole against the
 block the defender would make

The free-for-all attack comparison estimated each defender's damage as if
every attacker were blocked by every eligible blocker at once (CR 509.1a: a
creature blocks one attacker), and dropped any attacker whose best single
block was unfavourable. One untapped creature therefore stopped a whole
attack on paper: three 3/3s never attacked an opponent holding one 2/4.

Each defender's proposal is now re-scored as one attack against the block
the AI's own blocker heuristic would declare (neutral profile, restricted to
the blockers the comparison found legal, minimum-blocker floors included):
unblocked attackers deal their damage, trades and chumps are credited once.
Declined attackers are then added in order of unblocked damage, keeping the
best prefix, so attackers that outnumber the blockers get through. Declined
commanders are never added (CR 903.8). Two-player combat is unchanged, and
the old estimate is kept when the re-scoring would exceed its work bound.

Deck-lab traces (39 four-player precon games, Easy): the AI declared 48% of
the creatures the engine offered as attackers, and games ran a median 13
rounds against 9 in real games. See commander-decks
docs/lab/aitempo-2026-10-20.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
